Review corrections — events, models, memory and usage (B1-B7)

What changed:
- A terminal event now removes its tracked flow even when the last subscribed extension was removed mid-turn, preventing the 64-flow deadlock.
- Rust counts event.emit as delivered only when the Host explicitly returns queued:true; false or malformed acknowledgements are counted as dropped.
- All event activity counters, including activeHandlers, are clamped before crossing the JavaScript boundary.
- The provider usage migration test now decodes a complete pre-extension provider-usage.json fixture and asserts historical totals plus the default Extension workload.
- The memory budget test creates a real topic, spends the shared native budget, then proves memory.read omits content and memory.list returns a continuation cursor.
- Ollama done_reason is propagated to the shared finish-reason mapping, so token-limit completion is reported as length.
- Invalid model size/token bounds now surface as request failures, not permission denials, with a discriminating unit test.

Root-cause choices:
- Empty delivery routing consumes only an existing terminal sequence; it never creates a flow when nobody is subscribed.
- The Host acknowledgement is fail-closed: missing queued is not treated as success.
- Event sequence logic moved to a focused child module because event_delivery.rs crossed the 230-line source limit.

